 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Aim: to grow new plants from stem cuttings.\nMaterials: healthy mature stems of cassava (or hibiscus), a sharp cutlass or knife, a nursery bed or pots with moist soil.\nMethod:\n1. Choose a healthy, mature stem from a good plant.\n2. Cut pieces about 20–25 cm long, each with several nodes (buds), with a slanting cut.\n3. Plant each piece the right way up (buds pointing up), slanted, with about two thirds in the soil.\n4. Water regularly and protect from animals.\n5. Observe for three to four weeks and record when roots and shoots appear.\nResults: roots grow from the nodes under the soil; shoots grow from the buds above it.\nConclusion: a piece of stem with nodes can grow into a whole new plant, identical to its parent.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "c": "cassava"
  },
  "problem": "Why must a {c} cutting have nodes, and why is it planted the right way up?",
  "steps": [
   "New roots and shoots grow from the nodes (buds); a piece without nodes cannot grow.",
   "Buds pointing up grow into shoots towards the light; upside down, the cutting grows poorly or dies."
  ],
  "answer": "Nodes carry the buds; the right way up lets shoots grow upwards.",
  "checks": []
 },
